# ssh :: remote shells, keys, tunnels, file copies

## Connect
ssh <user>@<host> :: log in (the mesh: ssh lucien@x230.mesh)
ssh -p <port> <host> :: another port
ssh <host> '<cmd>' :: run one command, print its output
ssh -t <host> '<cmd>' :: same, with a terminal (sudo, htop)
ssh -J <jump> <host> :: through a jump host
mosh <host> :: survives roaming and sleep (needs mosh on both ends)
~. :: (after Enter) kill a frozen session; `~?` lists escapes

## Keys
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "<comment>" :: new key pair (~/.ssh/id_ed25519)
ssh-copy-id <user>@<host> :: install your public key there
ssh-add :: load the key into the agent (one passphrase per session)
ssh-add -l :: keys in the agent
ssh-keygen -p -f ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 :: change a key's passphrase
ssh-keygen -R <host> :: forget a host key (after a reinstall)
ssh-keygen -lf <file.pub> :: fingerprint of a key

## ~/.ssh/config
Host x230 :: an alias: then just `ssh x230`
  HostName x230.mesh :: the real address
  User lucien :: default user
  Port 22 :: and port
  IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 :: which key
  ProxyJump <jump> :: always through a jump host
  ForwardAgent yes :: use your agent on the remote (trusted hosts only)
Host * :: defaults for every host (put it last)
  ServerAliveInterval 30 :: keepalive, against idle drops

## Tunnels
ssh -L 8080:localhost:80 <host> :: local 8080 -> the remote's port 80
ssh -L 5432:<db>:5432 <host> :: reach a machine behind the host
ssh -R 9000:localhost:3000 <host> :: the remote's 9000 -> your port 3000
ssh -D 1080 <host> :: SOCKS proxy on localhost:1080 (browser through the host)
ssh -N -f -L <...> <host> :: tunnel only, in the background

## Files
scp <file> <host>:<dir>/ :: copy there (scp -r for directories)
scp <host>:<path> . :: copy back
rsync -avP <dir>/ <host>:<dir>/ :: sync a tree, resumable, with progress
rsync -avP --delete <src>/ <host>:<dst>/ :: mirror (deletes extra files there)
sftp <host> :: interactive file browser
sshfs <host>:<dir> <mountpoint> :: mount a remote directory

## Multiplexing (fast repeat connections)
ControlMaster auto :: (in Host *) reuse one connection
ControlPath ~/.ssh/cm-%r@%h:%p :: where its socket lives
ControlPersist 10m :: keep it open 10 minutes after the last session
ssh -O exit <host> :: close the shared connection

## Server side
sudo systemctl status sshd :: is it running
journalctl -u sshd -f :: login attempts, live
/etc/ssh/sshd_config :: PasswordAuthentication no, PermitRootLogin no
sudo sshd -t :: check the config before restarting
> keep a second session open while changing sshd: a mistake can lock you out
